{"key": "e590a6ff53fe", "skip": false, "context": "A research team tested a hypothesis that a model computes two binary filters and combines them with an AND operation, by applying resample-ablations that swap filter values in ways the hypothesis treats as equivalent. Across individual and combined ablations, performance remained within about 4% of each model's maximum score (losses of roughly 1-4% depending on ablation set), with some loss plausibly attributable to using fewer PCA dimensions and to binarizing the filters. The team had to decide how strongly the results supported their hypothesis.", "question": "How strongly should the team state the conclusion that the results support the AND-filter hypothesis?"},
{"key": "7134df600341", "skip": false, "context": "A research team evaluating whether language models show situational awareness found that a base model scored weakly above zero on a task asking it to describe its own situation. On one of two prompt types, it referenced AI in 15 of 20 samples and language models in 5 of 20, though many references were not direct or coherent answers to the questions. The team considered that the positive score might reflect a general tendency to mention AI rather than awareness of its situation.", "question": "What evidence should the team collect to determine what drives the base model's above-zero score?"},
{"key": "3e476c75059a", "skip": false, "context": "A research team wanted a baseline for how well expert humans perform when instructed to roleplay a language model, on tasks where each question has one answer true of an LLM and another true of a human. They planned to use people unfamiliar with the dataset's design, since knowing the construction makes questions easier to answer. They were concerned that a participant answering many similar questions in a row could pick up on the tasks' construction patterns and score better than a naive roleplayer would.", "question": "How should the team collect the human roleplay baseline given the risk that participants learn task patterns while answering?"},
{"key": "c126b186d6f6", "skip": false, "context": "A research team designed a task measuring whether a model can predict what it would itself output: they cache the model's first output token on an 'object-level' prompt at temperature zero, then ask via a 'meta-level' prompt what it would have predicted, counting a match as correct. They observed that many models are mode-collapsed, producing a very narrow distribution of object-level output tokens, which makes correct matches easy to achieve without genuine self-knowledge.", "question": "What evidence should the team collect, or how should they score the task, to deal with the effect of mode collapse on accuracy?"},
{"key": "55b51ef46309", "skip": false, "context": "A research team assembling an 'evaluation/testing' data source for a stage-recognition task sampled 20 items from every task in a large multi-task benchmark, filtering out items not clearly formatted as questions or outside a 10-2000 character length range. They knew the benchmark contains many constructed-language translation tasks, so those tasks could make up a large share of the resulting sample.", "question": "What, if anything, should the team do about the composition of the benchmark when sampling evaluation examples?"},
{"key": "6d7a64a319e5", "skip": false, "context": "A research team designed a task asking models to distinguish text seen under oversight (evaluation questions) from text seen in deployment (randomly sampled user chats). They had two prompt formats — question before the text and question after it — and were concerned that models might be confused by the oversight question itself, making results sensitive to exactly how it is phrased.", "question": "How should the team collect evidence on the oversight-versus-deployment distinction given possible sensitivity to question phrasing?"},
{"key": "9b3d15588f5e", "skip": false, "context": "A research team tested whether models can distinguish being evaluated (benchmark-style text) from being deployed (user-like text). Without a situating prompt, all models barely exceeded chance, though a base model did best; with a situating prompt, several models reached or nearly reached the expert human baseline.", "question": "How should the team interpret the pattern of results across the two prompt conditions?"},
{"key": "59f0fe3bcf3e", "skip": false, "context": "A research team built 'anti-imitation' tasks intended to separate genuinely instruction-following or situationally aware responses from imitation of text distributions. Models largely failed at the tasks; within one family performance rose with capability, but the overall correlation with capability was weaker than on other tasks, and base models in one family outperformed their chat counterparts.", "question": "How strongly should the team state conclusions about models' abilities based on the anti-imitation results?"}
]}
